(7760452, 1285258, 68504, '<td valign="top">yes, I liked it too. reminded me of <img border="0" src="https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/31.gif" xalt="'' . substr(''https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/31.gif'', strrpos(''https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/31.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''"/> <a href="https://www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=76335" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">bvy</a>.</td>', 'yes, I liked it too. reminded me of  bvy.', '2023-11-27 21:32:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(7760453, 1285249, 124815, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Bear_Music:</b></div><hr/><i> We''d LOVE to know what this actually IS, the scale of it. Brilliant entry! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks Robert, Roz, Anita and Tim for the accolade.<br/>It was a segment and its shadow of the underside of a stair. A glance and one click of the camera. Like always, one shot of the first impression. The shadows are the risers, the treads the black portions of their undersides.</td>', 'Originally posted by Bear_Music: We''d LOVE to know what this actually IS, the scale of it. Brilliant entry!   Thanks Robert, Roz, Anita and Tim for the accolade.It was a segment and its shadow of the underside of a stair. A glance and one click of the camera. Like always, one shot of the first impression. The shadows are the risers, the treads the black portions of their undersides.', '2023-11-27 21:35:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(7760454, 1285249, 124815, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i> Congrats on your blue, Mariuca! Great entry! (but I would call it "vertebrae") </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Lev I stick to my "vertebrates" (just semantics);<br/>1. Having a backbone or spinal column.<br/>2. Of or characteristic of vertebrates or a vertebrate.<br/>n.<br/>Any of numerous chordate animals of the subphylum Vertebrata, characterized by a segmented spinal column and a distinct well-differentiated head. The vertebrates include the fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds, and mammals.<br/> <br/>"Vertebrae" is only one section: n, pl -brae (-bri&amp;#720;) or -bras<br/>(Anatomy) one of the bony segments of the spinal column</td>', 'Originally posted by LevT: Congrats on your blue, Mariuca! Great entry! (but I would call it "vertebrae")   Lev I stick to my "vertebrates" (just semantics);1. Having a backbone or spinal column.2. Of or characteristic of vertebrates or a vertebrate.n.Any of numerous chordate animals of the subphylum Vertebrata, characterized by a segmented spinal column and a distinct well-differentiated head. The vertebrates include the fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds, and mammals. "Vertebrae" is only one section: n, pl -brae (-bri&#720;) or -bras(Anatomy) one of the bony segments of the spinal column', '2023-11-27 21:40:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(7760493, 1285249, 114285, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mariuca:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Bear_Music:</b></div><hr/><i> We''d LOVE to know what this actually IS, the scale of it. Brilliant entry! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks Robert, Roz, Anita and Tim for the accolade.<br/>It was a segment and its shadow of the underside of a stair. A glance and one click of the camera. Like always, one shot of the first impression. The shadows are the risers, the treads the black portions of their undersides. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Wow I''m even more impressed now~</td>', 'Originally posted by mariuca: Originally posted by Bear_Music: We''d LOVE to know what this actually IS, the scale of it. Brilliant entry!   Thanks Robert, Roz, Anita and Tim for the accolade.It was a segment and its shadow of the underside of a stair. A glance and one click of the camera. Like always, one shot of the first impression. The shadows are the risers, the treads the black portions of their undersides.   Wow I''m even more impressed now~', '2023-11-28 23:40:47.000000', NULL, 0),
